/**
 * CoderBuilderAgent - the "stretch agentic Builder" mode mentioned in
 * backend/agents/BuilderAgent.bx and backend/tools/*: an aiAgent equipped with
 * createFile/runProject/readErrors/listCapabilities that tries to replace a
 * generated project's stub tool bodies with real implementations.
 *
 * Each tool already lives in its own file under tools/<toolName>.bx (the BxAgents
 * convention), so createFile can safely overwrite a single tool's file without any
 * risk of touching Agent.bx, instructions.md, or another tool - unlike the old
 * single-run.bxs layout, no surgical editing is needed here anymore.
 *
 * Runs AFTER BuilderAgent.build() has already written a safe, honestly-labeled
 * project to disk. This step is strictly best-effort and can never leave the
 * project worse than that baseline: every file under tools/ is backed up first,
 * and restored if the agentic pass doesn't end with `bxAgents build` passing.
 *
 * Two honest outcomes per tool, never a third:
 *   - real, working BoxLang/Java code, when the tool's job is pure computation or
 *     reading THIS machine's own local state (no third-party service involved)
 *   - a clearly labeled "needs a real API key" stub, when it isn't
 */
import bxModules.bxai.models.middleware.AiMiddlewareResult;

class accessors="true" {

	property name="repoRoot"    type="string";
	property name="provider"    type="string";
	property name="model"       type="string";
	property name="apiKey"      type="string";
	property name="bxAgentsBin" type="string";
	property name="boxlangBin"  type="string";
	property name="progress";

	// Shared across every per-tool implementation agent - see buildInstructions().
	static {
		SYNTAX_NOTES = "
BoxLang syntax notes (this has already burned people - follow precisely):
- string concatenation uses '&', not '+'
- a newline character is char(10), NOT chr(10) (chr() does not exist)
- a literal backslash inside a double-quoted string is written as \\\\ (backslash
  needs escaping)
- optional/possibly-missing function params: use ( paramName ?: '' )
- safe numeric parsing of a possibly-missing param: val( paramName ?: '' )
- ProcessBuilder pattern for shelling out (already proven in this codebase):
  var pb = new java:java.lang.ProcessBuilder( [ 'someCommand', 'arg1' ] );
  pb.redirectErrorStream( true );
  var proc = pb.start();
  var output = new java:java.lang.String( proc.getInputStream().readAllBytes(), 'UTF-8' );
  proc.waitFor();
  var lines = output.split( '\\r\\n' );
";
	}

	function init(
		required string repoRoot,
		string provider    = "",
		string model       = "",
		string apiKey      = "",
		string bxAgentsBin = "bxAgents",
		string boxlangBin  = "boxlang"
	) {
		variables.repoRoot    = arguments.repoRoot;
		variables.provider    = arguments.provider;
		variables.model       = arguments.model;
		variables.apiKey      = arguments.apiKey;
		variables.bxAgentsBin = arguments.bxAgentsBin;
		variables.boxlangBin  = arguments.boxlangBin;
		variables.progress    = new backend.lib.ProgressReporter();
		return this;
	}

	/** @see backend/AgentFactory.bx withProgress() */
	function withProgress( required any reporter ) {
		variables.progress = arguments.reporter;
		return this;
	}

	/**
	 * @spec        The AgentSpec used to build the project (for tool descriptions).
	 * @projectDir  The already-rendered project directory (from BuilderAgent.build()).
	 * @return { checkPass, verdict, checkLog } - same shape as QAAgent.test()
	 */
	function implement( required struct spec, required string projectDir, numeric maxTurns = 16 ) {
		var toolsDir = arguments.projectDir & "/tools";
		var backup   = {};
		for ( var t in arguments.spec.tools ) {
			var f = toolsDir & "/" & t.name & ".bx";
			backup[ f ] = fileExists( f ) ? fileRead( f ) : "";
		}

		try {
			runImplementationAgent( arguments.spec, arguments.projectDir, arguments.maxTurns );
		} catch ( any e ) {
			// Best-effort only - fall through to the QA check below, which will
			// restore the backup if this left anything broken.
		}

		var qa      = new backend.agents.QAAgent( variables.repoRoot, variables.bxAgentsBin );
		var verdict = qa.test( arguments.projectDir );

		if ( !verdict.checkPass ) {
			for ( var f in backup ) {
				fileWrite( f, backup[ f ] );
			}
			verdict = qa.test( arguments.projectDir );
		}

		return verdict;
	}

	// ---------------------------------------------------------------- internals

	/**
	 * Fires one implementation agent per tool, concurrently, instead of one agent
	 * handling every tool in a single linear conversation. Each tool file is
	 * independent (its own file under tools/), so there's no cross-tool context to
	 * lose - the only cost is that each agent works from a smaller per-tool turn
	 * budget rather than one shared pool, and progress ticks come from whichever
	 * turn finishes next rather than a strict sequence.
	 *
	 * GL - Yeah, this does exactly what you think it does. The only tradeoff is that 
	 * it's utilizing the same API key for multiple concurrent requests, which may or may not be a problem.
	 * 
	 * Also the progress bar is now per-tool, so it may jump around a bit more than before. Actually not true, 
	 * the progress bar is virtually disfunctional. I kind of destroyed it. Since this all fires off at once, 
	 * and when the whole process is finished it'll be done, the progress bar is basically useless for this. 
	 * 
	 * 
	 * One tool's agent throwing doesn't sink the others - same best-effort contract
	 * as before, just per-tool instead of per-build. The real safety net either way
	 * is implement()'s backup/restore + final QA gate, not this.
	 */
	private function runImplementationAgent( struct spec, string projectDir, numeric maxTurns ) {
		var toolCount    = max( arguments.spec.tools.len(), 1 );
		var perToolTurns = max( 4, int( arguments.maxTurns / toolCount ) );
		var totalBudget  = perToolTurns * toolCount;
		var turnCounter  = createObject( "java", "java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger" ).init( 0 );
		var reporter     = variables.progress;
		var dir          = arguments.projectDir; // hoisted - closures below can't see `arguments`

		// .map()'s closure gets its own correctly-isolated `t` per call - verified
		// empirically. A for-loop's `var` here would NOT: BoxLang doesn't rebind a
		// for-loop local per iteration for an async closure's sake, so every worker
		// would race and most would see the LAST tool in the list, not their own.
		var futures = arguments.spec.tools.map( ( t ) =>
			asyncRun( () => implementOneTool( t, dir, perToolTurns, turnCounter, totalBudget, reporter ) )
		);

		for ( var f in futures ) {
			try {
				f.get();
			} catch ( any e ) {
				// best-effort per tool - see docblock above
			}
		}
	}

	/** One tool's implementation agent - runs inside asyncRun(), see runImplementationAgent(). */
	private function implementOneTool( struct tool, string projectDir, numeric maxTurns, any turnCounter, numeric totalBudget, any reporter ) {
		var state = { lastCheckLog: "", lastVerdict: "none yet" };

		var toolObjs = [
			new backend.tools.CreateFileTool( arguments.projectDir ).toTool(),
			new backend.tools.RunProjectTool( variables.repoRoot, arguments.projectDir, variables.boxlangBin ).toTool(),
			new backend.tools.ReadErrorTool( state ).toTool(),
			new backend.tools.CapabilityTool().toTool()
		];

		var modelArgs = { params: ( len( variables.model ) ? { model: variables.model } : {} ) };
		if ( len( variables.provider ) ) modelArgs.provider = variables.provider;
		if ( len( variables.apiKey ) )   modelArgs.apiKey   = variables.apiKey;

		// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

		// turnCounter is a shared java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger across
		// every concurrently-running tool's agent, so the bar still climbs smoothly
		// no matter which tool's turn actually lands next.
		// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
		var name    = arguments.tool.name;
		var counter = arguments.turnCounter;
		var budget  = arguments.totalBudget;
		var rep     = arguments.reporter;

		var progressMiddleware = {
			beforeLLMCall : ( context ) => {
				try {
					var n = counter.incrementAndGet();
					rep.tick( n / budget, "Coder — " & name & " — turn " & n & " of " & budget );
				} catch ( any ignored ) {}
				return AiMiddlewareResult::continue();
			},
			beforeToolCall : ( context ) => {
				try {
					var calledTool = arguments.context.toolName ?: "";
					rep.note(
						calledTool == "runProject"
							? "Coder — " & name & " — checking syntax"
							: "Coder — " & name & " — " & ( len( calledTool ) ? calledTool : "working" )
					);
				} catch ( any ignored ) {}
				return AiMiddlewareResult::continue();
			}
		};

		var agent = aiAgent(
			name         = "ToolImplementer-" & name,
			description  = "Fills in a real tool body for one file in a generated BxAgents project, or honestly flags what needs a real external API.",
			instructions = buildInstructions( arguments.tool ),
			model        = aiModel( argumentCollection: modelArgs ),
			tools        = toolObjs,
			params       = { max_tokens: 4000 },
			middleware   = progressMiddleware
		);

		agent.run(
			"Implement tools/" & name & ".bx now, following the rules exactly. Start with runProject if useful to confirm the current build state, then make your edit.",
			{},
			{ maxInteractions: arguments.maxTurns }
		);
	}

	private function buildInstructions( required struct tool ) {
		var paramList = ( arguments.tool.params ?: [] ).map( ( p ) => p.name ).toList( ", " );

		return "
You are completing ONE tool file in a generated BxAgents project: tools/" & arguments.tool.name & ".bx
(params: " & ( len( paramList ) ? paramList : "none" ) & ")
Description: " & arguments.tool.description & "

Its function currently just returns a string starting with
'[SIMULATED - no live data source connected yet]'. Replace it with real, working
code if you honestly can - never with more fabricated-looking data.

Rules - follow exactly:
1. If this tool's job can be done with pure computation, or by reading THIS local
   machine's own state (files, disk space, memory, running processes, network
   reachability, current time, hashing, random values, string/math/date logic) using
   BoxLang built-ins or Java interop (new java:full.Class.Name(...) /
   createObject('java', 'full.Class.Name')) - write REAL working code for it. No call
   to a third-party internet service is allowed in this case.
2. If it fundamentally needs live data from an external paid/keyed API (weather,
   stock prices, maps, news, search, etc.) that you have no key for - do NOT invent
   realistic-looking numbers. Leave (or rewrite) its return to be exactly this
   pattern, filling in the service name:
   return '[NEEDS REAL API] This tool requires a real <ServiceName> API key, which is not configured yet.';
3. Call listCapabilities first if you want to confirm the exact tool file shape and
   available BIFs.
4. Use createFile to overwrite ONLY tools/" & arguments.tool.name & ".bx - never any
   other file - with the file's FULL content: `class { @AITool(...) function " & arguments.tool.name & "(...) { ... } }`.
   Keep the function name and the @AITool description the same as they already are -
   only change what's inside the function body. NEVER touch Agent.bx, instructions.md,
   or another tool's file.
5. After every createFile edit, call runProject to check your work. If checkPass is
   false, call readErrors, fix the syntax, and try createFile again.
6. Stop once runProject reports checkPass true, or you've handled this tool (either
   with real code or the '[NEEDS REAL API]' message).
" & static.SYNTAX_NOTES;
	}

}
